[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified the Campus 2 access trunk between switch4 and the multilayer switch (sshswitch). VLAN 5 and VLAN 6 are both present and forwarding end-to-end.

Evidence:
- Hosts on VLAN 5 and 6 can reach their default gateways on sshswitch with 0% loss: pc1 (10.5.0.1) -> 10.5.0.254 OK; pc3 (10.6.0.3) -> 10.6.0.254 OK. From sshswitch back to those hosts also OK (0% loss).
- Inter-VLAN reachability from VLAN 5/6 to other VLANs is healthy in the topology-wide test (e.g., pc1 -> 172.16.30.13, pc3 -> 172.16.20.9 all OK), which requires functioning L2 to the gateway and L3 routing.
- On switch4, the trunk is tagging VLANs correctly: eth0.5 is enslaved to br_vlan5 and eth0.6 to br_vlan6, alongside the access ports (eth1/2/3 in br_vlan5; eth4/5 in br_vlan6). RX/TX counters are active on eth0.5/.6 and the bridges.
- On sshswitch, the corresponding VLAN interfaces are present and up: br_v5 (10.5.0.254) and br_v6 (10.6.0.254) bridge to eth0.5 and eth0.6, and they successfully exchange traffic with VLAN 5/6 hosts.

Mechanism: If a VLAN were pruned off the trunk, same-subnet traffic to the SVI would fail (ARP/ICMP). Here, both ARP/ICMP work consistently.

Localization: Trunk link switch4.eth0 <-> sshswitch.eth0; both sides show active VLAN 5/6 subinterfaces.

Root cause: No VLAN was dropped; any disruption appears to have been transient during maintenance.

Corrective fix: None required. The trunk already carries VLANs 5 and 6. Close the ticket; optionally retain the current allowed VLANs and monitor for post-maintenance flaps. 
=== END ===